# A2 external-supervisor terminate/reconcile source implementation — 2026-09-15

Status: **IMPLEMENTED IN SOURCE; NON-LIVE TESTS PASS; LIVE AUTHORITY NOT YET QUALIFIED**. The running external supervisor PID 30193 was neither restarted, replaced nor modified as a process. A host-authorized restart is required to load this source. A2 and E1-B01 remain blocked; E1-WP-001 remains ineligible and was not dispatched. A2.1 and the controller-owned execution workspace remain accepted as PASS.

The new `terminate_reconcile` broker request contains only the existing protocol, broker, session, authorization, ActionRequest and ExecutionScope identities plus a single-use `recovery_request_id`. The socket handler rejects additional fields, including PID, cgroup path and caller timeout. `BrokerProtocol` accepts this operation only with its fixed invocation tuple. Broker-created scopes persist that owner tuple in supervisor-owned audit evidence; termination requires an exact match to it and a scope ID of the production `scope-<32 hex>` form. A consumed recovery ID remains consumed across supervisor audit replay. Unowned legacy scopes cannot use this operation. Scope creation now also rejects absolute and traversing scope paths.

`HostScopeSupervisor` closes admission before termination and selects only its stored cgroup under `/sys/fs/cgroup/unified/kge-forge/executor/<scope>`. `TerminationEngine` reads `cgroup.procs` from the scope and bounded descendant cgroups, then independently reads the root `cgroup.events` population value. Contradictory, missing, symlinked or oversized observations are INDETERMINATE. A post-QUIESCENT population anomaly is INDETERMINATE rather than silently reopened.

The host-owned signaler opens a pidfd for each observed member and checks `/proc/<pid>/cgroup` against the fixed authorized `0::/kge-forge/executor/<scope>` path before pidfd signaling. It sends bounded SIGTERM, then SIGKILL to surviving observed members. Python 3.8 on this x86_64 host uses the Linux pidfd syscalls when Python wrappers are unavailable; an unavailable or failed pidfd operation is INDETERMINATE. It never uses `os.kill(pid)` or a caller-supplied PID. Delivery, ESRCH or process disappearance does not establish quiescence: after each phase the supervisor re-reads cgroup membership and population. It reports QUIESCENT only after admission is CLOSED and both authoritative member count and `populated` are zero. Survivors, uncertain identity, observation failure and window expiry return explicit INDETERMINATE while admission remains closed.

The host audit at `<socket>.supervisor-audit.jsonl` is outside the payload mount, opened mode 0600 with fsync. It records owner creation, authorization or denial, consumed recovery ID, admission closure, every member/population observation, signal intent before each pidfd action, delivery outcome, and final state. Audit replay restores owner and consumed IDs but starts every adopted scope INDETERMINATE, without admitting work or inferring quiescence from a prior terminal record. A fresh recovery request must inspect the kernel again.

Non-live qualification used fake cgroup files and a fake signaler; no real PID was signaled. Twelve bounded termination tests passed: valid termination and reconciliation, mismatched identity/scope, generic PID/cgroup-field rejection, stale/replayed recovery across audit restore, already-quiescent re-observation, descendant membership, surviving member, observation failure, delivery/disappearance without quiescence, post-quiescence population anomaly, pidfd outside-scope rejection, and adopted active-scope reconciliation. The full adapter suite passed **35 tests**; `python3 -m py_compile` and `git diff --check` passed. Source changed: `adapter/host_supervisor.py`, new `adapter/termination.py`, `adapter/supervisor_server.py`, `adapter/launch_broker.py`, and new bounded tests. Fabrik and kge-forge-demo were untouched.

Live qualification remains required after a host-authorized restart. The host must first verify the authorized PID/socket/cgroup identity and that pre-upgrade scope directories are empty or otherwise accounted for: pre-upgrade scopes have no new-format owner audit record and cannot be adopted by this implementation. Then qualify pidfd support and an identity-bound stubborn/detached-child stop through `production_spawn`, confirm authoritative `CLOSED → member zero / populated 0 → QUIESCENT`, test denial/replay and uncertain observations, and reconcile an interrupted controller turn without permitting dependent execution. The Forge controller's durable action replay and global single-invocation ownership remain separate A2 recovery/release work; this source implementation alone does not close those gates.
